From 2773c9c9c05bab5f2a2ead77773a143af11320d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 00:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Detect operator dependencies per channel, transitively,
 including required APIs

Dependency detection read only olm.package.required, only from the
newest bundle of the default channel, one level deep, and gave added
dependencies a guessed "stable-<x.y>" channel when none was known.

catalog_metadata.py now also writes dependency-graph.json: for every
package and channel, the requirements of the channel head. Required
APIs (olm.gvk.required) are resolved against the olm.gvk APIs bundles in
the catalog provide: exactly one provider becomes a dependency
(reason "api"); several or none are listed as unresolvedApis with their
candidates. operators.json and dependencies.json keep their shape, and
the Dockerfile copies the new file.

GET /api/operators/:operator/dependencies takes an optional channel,
walks the graph transitively (each dependency through its own default
channel) and returns requiredBy, the real defaultChannel and
unresolvedApis. Catalog data without the graph falls back to
dependencies.json (resolution "legacy").

The UI asks again when a package's channel changes, uses the real
default channel (or no channel filter instead of a guessed name), and
shows unresolved APIs as a warning. A package with no channel filter
leaves "channels" out of the YAML.

Tests: a synthetic FBC catalog (tests/fixtures/fbc-dependency-graph)
covers channel heads, API resolution, ambiguous/unprovided/self-provided
APIs and transitive dependencies; the generated graph must match the
server fixture. The five new API tests fail on the previous server.

